 \subsection{Discussion}
 \label{sec:Discussion}
 
-The main result has two parts. First, GEP found formulas with substantially smaller average gaps than the fixed rules on held-out and extrapolated Jooken cases. The matched random control shows that evolutionary selection mattered. Second, the richer FULL alphabet did not meaningfully improve the predeclared primary comparison with BASE. A result can therefore support the learning method while rejecting the proposed representation improvement.
-
-The mean advantage over MAXPW needs care. FULL lost on most sibling groups but avoided several large gaps. It was better for mean loss, not for every instance or exact-solution frequency. A user prioritizing the median or exact recovery might choose differently.
-
-Tuning reduced the risk of an arbitrary parameter choice, but this study did not define a formal untuned control. The selected survivor had a pooled validation gap of 5,486$\ppm$; four alternatives scored 5,540, 5,555, 5,672, and 6,908$\ppm$. Three were close, and one was clearly weaker. It is therefore fair to say that tuning protected the experiment from a poor setting, but not to claim an exact counterfactual gain. Validation is selection data after repeated use; only the sealed test supplies the final evidence.
-
-Other limits are direct. We previously inspected the Jooken collection; the row-order audit found sensitivity; the alphabets differ in size; and the frozen rules failed zero-shot Pisinger transfer. A next study should train and validate on Pisinger classes or on a deliberately mixed benchmark, then compare domain-specific and multi-domain rules under the same protocol. Retraining may help, but the present data cannot say by how much or whether it would surpass MAXPW.
+The original study supports learning compact Jooken rules, while rejecting a meaningful FULL-over-BASE representation benefit. The additional comparison supports lower selected-rule mean gaps than the reimplemented kNN selector for three formulae, conditional on the fixed models and Jooken development distribution. SCALE's interval and kNN's better median prevent a uniform superiority claim.
+
+The mean advantages reflect protection against large gaps, not success on most groups or dominance in exact recovery. Applications prioritizing the median or exact solutions may prefer another rule.
+
+Tuning reduced the risk of an arbitrary setting, but we did not define a formal untuned control. The five full-budget survivors varied in validation quality; their comparison supports the selection procedure, not an exact counterfactual gain from tuning. Validation remains selection data after repeated use.
+
+Limits remain: prior inspection of Jooken, row-order sensitivity, unequal alphabet sizes, and failed zero-shot Pisinger transfer. Retraining on Pisinger or mixed benchmarks needs a separate locked study; these data cannot predict its benefit or whether it would surpass MAXPW.
 
 \section{Conclusion and Future Work}
 \label{sec:Conclusion}
 
-GEP can evolve short, inspectable priority expressions with lower held-out mean gaps than DEF, MAXP, MAXPW, and MINW on the hard Jooken collection. The result held up across new item-count and capacity levels from that generator and benefited from evolutionary search rather than random expression sampling. The proposed capacity and scale terminals, however, did not improve the simpler BASE representation by a practically meaningful amount in the primary test. The cost, row-order sensitivity, skewed win pattern, and failed zero-shot transfer keep the conclusion narrow: these are useful learned Jooken rules, not universal knapsack heuristics.
-
-Future work will investigate order-invariant representations and decoders to remove the dependence on the original item ordering. We also plan to train and evaluate domain-specific and multi-domain rules on mixed Jooken and Pisinger benchmarks under the same locked protocol. Finally, we could explore adaptive or ensemble strategies to select among complementary evolved rules based on instance characteristics.
+GEP evolved inspectable rules with lower held-out mean gaps than four fixed rules on hard Jooken instances, with gains across new parameter levels and over random search. The richer terminals did not meaningfully improve BASE. Three selected formulae also improve mean gap over the revision kNN comparator, under the stated training distribution and conditional inference. Computation cost, order sensitivity, uneven wins, and failed transfer restrict these findings to the tested family.
+
+Future work will study order-invariant representations and decoders, domain-specific and multi-domain training on mixed Jooken/Pisinger benchmarks, and adaptive selection among complementary learned rules under locked evaluation.
 
